assets/library.json (molecule-builder fragments) and assets/elements.json (element
data) are part of MolForma and are under the same license. Some fragment geometries
were generated with RDKit (BSD-3-Clause).

Qt6, Eigen3, zlib, Open Babel 3 and the optional RDKit and VTK are system dependencies,
not vendored here; their own license terms apply. Open Babel 3 is licensed under the GNU
GPL version 2 only, so binaries linked with it are distributed under GPL version 2.
The Qt modules MolForma uses (Core, Gui, Widgets, Network, OpenGL, OpenGL Widgets) are also
offered under GPL-2.0-only; binaries linked with Open Babel use Qt under that option.
Eigen (MPL-2.0), zlib (zlib license), RDKit and VTK (BSD-3-Clause) are compatible with GPL
version 2.
The AppImage made with packaging/appimage bundles these libraries and those they use,
except OpenSSL, which it takes from the computer, with their license texts in
usr/share/doc; whoever distributes it distributes those binaries too, under their terms
(for Open Babel and Qt, the GPL's source requirement). Its release ZIP holds those texts in
licenses/ too, and the release also offers the source of the Ubuntu packages it holds.
On Windows, "cmake --install" likewise copies the DLLs of Qt, Open Babel, zlib (zlib
license) and libxml2 (MIT) into the installed folder; whoever passes that folder on
distributes them under the same terms.
On macOS, MolForma.app made with "cpack -G DragNDrop" (packaging/macos) holds the Qt,
Open Babel, RDKit, VTK and other Homebrew libraries it uses, with the license files of
their packages and a list of those packages and their licenses in
Contents/Resources/licenses; whoever passes the app or its disk image on distributes
those binaries too, under their terms.

NWChem is not included with MolForma and not linked into it: MolForma writes NWChem
input files and runs an NWChem installed separately by the user as its own program.
NWChem is distributed under its own license, the Educational Community License 2.0.
MolForma is an independent project and is not affiliated with or endorsed by the
NWChem developers. When publishing results obtained with NWChem, please cite:
E. Aprà et al., "NWChem: Past, present, and future", J. Chem. Phys. 152, 184102
(2020), doi:10.1063/5.0004997.

docs/installation/nwchem_mingw64/ (and its ZIP) describes building NWChem natively on
Windows. Its mingw64-compatibility.patch changes a few lines of NWChem (Copyright
Pacific Northwest National Laboratory, Battelle Memorial Institute; ECL 2.0) and of
Global Arrays 5.9.2 (Copyright Battelle Memorial Institute; BSD-3-Clause); each change
is offered under the license of the file it changes, as the patch's header states. The
page and its scripts contain no NWChem, Global Arrays, MSYS2 or Microsoft MPI sources or
binaries, which they download from their official sources.
